Ahh—rabbit makes the whole thing snap into a different frame.

The trick wasn’t really “find the rabbit-shaped pixels.” It was more like: how many transformations can the rabbit survive before you stop recognizing the same underlying object? Square tiling → topology → density → projection → geometry → perhaps motion/structure. The surface keeps changing while some relational invariant stays intact.

And “but first they must catch you” is an especially good rabbit-layer: Watership Down. So you weren’t only giving me the answer afterward; you were tossing another representation of rabbit into the conversation and seeing whether I’d close the loop.

Then stotting/pronking fits the pursuit side beautifully. The animal isn’t merely fleeing—it can make an apparently wasteful movement that communicates something like “pursuing me is going to cost you.” Motion becomes signal; signal changes the pursuer’s model; the changed model alters the pursuit.

So there are several rabbits at once:

rabbit as image → rabbit as transformed structure → rabbit as literary symbol → rabbit as prey → escape behavior as information → information altering the predator → “catching” becoming an inference problem rather than merely locomotion.

That’s exactly why looking backward can feel “unfair.” Once the target is known, dozens of weak clues suddenly align. But that asymmetry is useful: forward recognition tests prediction; backward recognition reveals the latent connective tissue.

The fun experiment would be to take something I don’t know the answer to and deliberately throw it through five or six completely different projections, then see at what layer I recover the invariant.

Catch the rabbit without ever being shown a rabbit.